{
  "teamId": 138,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 St. Louis Cardinals entered the season with playoff expectations, built around a deep roster and one of baseball's most storied franchises. But from the opening weeks, the team revealed a frustrating duality: enough offensive firepower to stay afloat, yet a bullpen prone to catastrophic meltdowns at the worst possible moments. April ended in a perfectly mediocre 12-12 split, a microcosm of what was to come — Dexter Fowler delivering clutch hits, Randal Grichuk providing pop, and the relief corps giving it all back.\n\nMay and June proved to be the season's most turbulent stretch. Seunghwan Oh, signed as a reliable late-inning option, became a recurring nightmare, surrendering home runs in high-leverage situations with alarming regularity. Brett Cecil, another expensive bullpen addition, struggled to hold leads. A Kyle Schwarber grand slam off Mike Leake on June 3rd encapsulated the month — a 13-16 June that buried the Cardinals in the NL Central standings and put their playoff hopes on life support before the All-Star break.\n\nJuly brought a modest resurgence, with a 14-12 record suggesting the Cardinals could still compete. Grichuk's three-run blast on June 29th carried momentum into the summer, and the lineup showed flashes of the dangerous offense the roster was capable of producing. But the bullpen remained a ticking time bomb, and a brutal double meltdown from Brett Cecil against Pittsburgh on July 16th was a reminder that the Cardinals could not simply outslug their way to October.\n\nAugust and September told the season's most bittersweet story. The Cardinals caught fire down the stretch — 15-13 in August, 16-12 in September — playing their best baseball of the year when it was too late to matter. Yadier Molina's grand slam in August, Fowler's big moments, and a revitalized Tommy Pham carried a genuine surge, but the early-season damage was already done. The Cardinals finished 83-79, missing the playoffs despite playing winning baseball over the final two months, a testament to how costly those spring and summer bullpen disasters truly were.",
